<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # That's a Purple Flower. – Lesson # 3</h2>
<p style="text-align: center;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors in Our World</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Purple Flower.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Name primary and secondary colors. Interactive: Express likes or dislikes using simple words and pointing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe objects using colors and simple sentences (e.g., "That's a blue bird."). Can answer questions about colors and objects, using one or two-word responses (e.g., "What color is it?" "Blue."). Can express likes and dislikes, using simple words and gestures (e.g., "I like red.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students with a "Color Song" (e.g., "The Color Song" or "I See Colors Everywhere"). Students sing along and point to colored objects in the classroom as the colors are mentioned (e.g., "Red, red, I see red!"). Then, the teacher shows large flashcards of objects learned in previous lessons (bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky). Teacher asks "What is this?" for choral response and "What color is it?" focusing on familiar colors (red, blue, green).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a purple flower. Teacher points and says clearly, "Look! That's a purple flower." Teacher repeats this a few times, emphasizing "purple" and the /l/ sound in "flower". Then, the teacher holds up a flashcard of a blue bird and says, "That's a blue bird." Teacher models "What color is it?" with rising intonation and answers, "It is purple." or "It is blue." Teacher also models simple expressions of likes: "I like red!" while pointing to a red object.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses concrete examples and gestures.
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the purple flower flashcard: "Is this a blue flower?" (No!) "What color is it?" (Purple!).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a red object: "Do I like blue?" (No!) "What color do I like?" (Red!).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors in Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful picture cards or a simple, illustrated poster/story with common objects and their colors, e.g., "My Colorful World." The material will feature objects like "bird," "flower," "cat," "tree," "house," "leaf," "sky" in different primary and secondary colors (red, blue, green, purple, orange).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying familiar objects by color (e.g., "blue bird" or "red flower."). Using simple color phrases in sentences (e.g., "That is a red bird.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> Teacher displays the poster. "Look at the picture! What do you see?" (Students point and say "bird," "flower," etc., or just point). "Are there many colors?" (Yes!).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Point and Say:</b> Teacher says, "Show me the green tree!" Students point to the green tree. "Show me the orange cat!" Students point. Teacher asks, "What color is the sky?" (Blue!).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Echo Repetition:</b> Teacher points to an object and says the sentence, "That's a red house." Students echo the sentence chorally, then individually. Focus on "color + noun" structure and /l/ sound in "blue" and "leaf".</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking: Naming primary and secondary colors using "That's a [color] [noun]." Answering "What color is it?" with one or two-word responses. Practicing /l/ phoneme in "blue" and "leaf", and initial sounds for /b/ in "bird", /k/ in "cat", /f/ in "flower".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Point (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher lays out flashcards of various colored objects (e.g., red bird, blue sky, green leaf, purple flower, orange cat, red house, green tree). The teacher calls out sentences, "Show me the blue sky!" or "Find the green leaf!" Students listen and point to the correct flashcard. Then, the teacher asks, "What color is it?" and students respond with the color name (e.g., "Blue!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. "What Color Is It?" Chain (Semi-controlled):</b> Students sit in a circle. The teacher starts by holding up a flashcard, for example, a red flower, and asks the student next to them, "What color is it?" The student responds, "Red!" or "It's red!" Then, that student takes another flashcard and asks the next student. The teacher circulates, correcting pronunciation and modeling, especially the /l/ sound in "blue" and "leaf", and initial phonemic awareness for /b/, /k/, /f/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Talk: "My Favorite Color" (Guided Interaction):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of 5-6 small flashcards of colored objects (e.g., a green bird, an orange flower, a purple cat). Student A picks a card and shows it to Student B. Student A asks, "What color is it?" Student B answers, "[Color]!" or "It's a [color] [object]!" Then Student A says, "I like [color]!" Student B repeats this. They swap roles. Teacher encourages pointing and simple responses.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors in Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Color Show and Tell" in small groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are divided into small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a basket of diverse colored objects (realia or flashcards: e.g., a small blue toy car, a green leaf, a red apple, a purple crayon, an orange ball, a yellow flower).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student picks one object from the basket.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students take turns showing their object to their group members.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A holds up their object (e.g., a purple crayon) and says, "That's a purple crayon." Then they say, "I like purple." or "I like it!" while pointing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The other students in the group can ask, "What color is it?" (Teacher guides if needed). Student A responds, "Purple!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, encouraging students to speak, providing prompts, and ensuring they use "color + noun" and express likes. Focus is on fluency and participation rather than perfect accuracy.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At the end, each group collectively counts the objects by color (e.g., "two blue birds," "three red flowers").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Color Show and Tell" and an individual "Color Identification" task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will individually point to a flashcard shown by the teacher and verbally state its color or describe it using a simple sentence. For example, teacher shows a red bird flashcard. Student says "Red!" or "That's a red bird." Teacher also shows an object and asks, "Do you like it?" Student responds "Yes, I do!" or "No, I don't!" or "I like [color]!" with pointing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target colors and nouns.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (colors, bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to use "That's a [color] [noun]" to describe an object.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to answer "What color is it?" with a one or two-word response.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to express "I like [color]" or "I like it" using simple words and gestures.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw their favorite colored object they saw today and say its color to a peer or the teacher before leaving. The teacher asks:
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color did you draw?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you like your drawing?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="margin-bottom: 4px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new color word did you say today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was fun to say with your friends today?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to name primary colors and use "That's a [color] [noun]" with modeling. Some struggled with "purple" and "orange." The group activity fostered good interaction, though some students were shy to speak individually. I need to reinforce "purple" and "orange" vocabulary and encourage more individual speaking practice using sentence frames. Providing more opportunities for expressing "likes/dislikes" will also be beneficial.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on the learned vocabulary and color-noun structures by having students label pictures and write simple sentences about colored objects. We will also review the phonemic awareness of initial sounds to support early writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>